  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1152 AM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nONE LAST RAINY DAY; DRYING OUT THIS WEEKEND: \n\nA low pressure system will track across the Intermountain west \nover the next 24 hours which will break us out of the ongoing \nwet pattern. Until then, expect one last day of scattered to \nwidespread showers and storms. Northeast Utah and northwest \nColorado in particular could be the hot spot for stronger storms\nwith stronger wind shear and instability expected. Isolated \nstrong storms cannot be ruled out elsewhere. With stronger \ndaytime heating expected today, storms will be capable of \nproducing strong outflow winds, hail, and heavy rainfall. The \nthreat for localized flash flooding will be heightened due to \nantecedent saturated soils and the potential for additional \nheavy rainfall. \n\nBy Saturday, the passage of a cold front will help scour some \nof the moisture from the region, but PWATs will remain around \n150% percent of normal. This means that the storm coverage and \nthe threat for heavy rainfall will decrease, but enough moisture\nremains in place to support isolated to scattered convection. \nSunday looks drier yet with PWATs 75-125% of normal. However, we\nexpect enough remnant moisture for isolated convection over the\nhigher terrain.\n\nMORE MONSOON NEXT WEEK:\n\nThe relatively dry period won't stick around for long as \nmoisture- rich southwesterly flow kicks back in Monday PM into \nTuesday. While the details could fluctuate between now and then \nwith variance amongst and within ensemble families, the general \nthought is that wetter weather will return by Tuesday and could \nremain in place throughout the week. Early guidance supports \nwetter weather, especially south of I-70, but less widespread \nthan we've experienced this week.",
